{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target's sufficiency claim misidentifies the logical structure of constraint. By stating that a test constrains its subject when the subject's acceptance is conditional on the test's failure being impossible, the target conflates the test being necessary for rejection (shown-to-fail-first) with it being sufficient for acceptance. The 1/9 mutation loss in compile.py under shown-to-fail-first installation directly falsifies this sufficiency: the test was installed and passed (failure impossible), yet the mutation was lost, meaning the subject was accepted despite not satisfying the test's constraint. The test thus functions as a heuristic or gatekeeping ritual rather than a boundary-setting condition. The target's mechanism—claiming the test sets a modal boundary—is contradicted by the fact that a mutation slipped through; a genuine sufficiency condition would block unfit subjects by definition. The target never specifies what additional conditions are necessary beyond the test's failure being impossible, leaving the 'constraint' under-specified to the point of vacuity: if a constraint permits 1/9 defects, it describes a statistical tendency, not a logical boundary.","counterexample":["A mutation was introduced into compile.py, the test suite was installed via shown-to-fail-first, and the test passed (failure was impossible), yet the mutation was lost."],"premise":"The 1/9 mutation loss under shown-to-fail-first installation falsifies the sufficiency claim: if the test constrained the subject, the mutation would have been caught, not lost.","premise_evidence":[{"block":"aa835741bebc4b4cb189f4b08bef649a","quote":"compile.py 1/9 mutations lost under shown-to-fail-first installation"}],"target":"0c9d02e6f822a69e82f7ce62d697fd1c59d84eef86e4644a475214b883cc95b6"}]}